<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agathocles of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">361–289 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agathocles of Syracuse seized power as tyrant of Syracuse in 317 BCE and later took the title of king. Fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, he boldly invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in 310 BCE. He extended his power over much of Sicily, raided southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and held Corcyra. His Campanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the Mamertines, seized Messana after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse.jpg" alt="Agathocles of Syracuse" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stella · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moneta_Apollo_di_Agatocle_IV-III_sec._a.C.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Agathocles was a son of Carcinus, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a> and settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermae-himeraeae">Thermae Himeraeae</a>, in the Carthaginian part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 343 BCE, when Agathocles was about eighteen, the family moved to Syracuse in answer to a call from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> for new citizens. The sources say Carcinus was a potter and that Agathocles followed him, though modern historians generally think he must have been wealthy and owned a workshop. Agathocles later advertised his low origins as a ruler. After Timoleon's death he rose to be a chiliarch under the general Damas, and he married Damas's widow, which made him one of the richest men in Syracuse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Syracuse was then ruled by an oligarchic club called the Six Hundred, and Agathocles sided with the democrats. After accusing the oligarchs Sosistratus and Heracleides of seeking tyranny, he left the city and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> life in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He later defeated a Syracusan force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a>, which led to the oligarchs' fall, but the Corinthian commander Acestorides exiled him again. He returned and was elected commander in chief in 319 or 318 BCE, swearing an oath that he would not establish a tyranny.</p><p>In 317 BCE he used a loyal force to launch a coup. His men massacred leading members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> more than 4,000 people were killed. He then secured election as general with unlimited power, the title Dionysius I had used, and announced a cancellation of debts and a redistribution of land. He brought many Sicilian cities under his hegemony. War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> followed, and after defeat on the Himera River in 311 BCE he was besieged in Syracuse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 310 BCE he landed at Cape Bon and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>. He allied with Ailymas, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, and later killed him, and he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophellas">Ophellas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> killed after luring him into the campaign. He was finally defeated in 307 BCE and fled secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, leaving his army and two sons, who were murdered. He made peace with Carthage in 306 BCE, which left him control of Sicily east of the Halycus, and took the royal title in 304 BCE.</p><p>His last years were marked by ill health and an attempt by his grandson Archagathus to seize the throne. Diodorus states that Agathocles was poisoned, but Justin and most modern historians say he died a natural death. Machiavelli cited him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> as a man who gained a principality through crimes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agathocles of Syracuse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Dionysius II of Syracuse was driven out by Timoleon in 344 BCE, and <b>Agathocles of Syracuse</b>, who seized power in 317 BCE, took the title of king in 304 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hellenistic period Corcyra was held by <b>Agathocles of Syracuse</b> and Pyrrhus of the Kingdom of Epirus.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcyra">Corcyra</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 4th century Croton suffered from Dionysius I, who held it from 379 BCE, and from the Bruttians and <b>Agathocles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/croton">Croton</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bruttii captured it around 356 BCE, and <b>Agathocles</b> briefly held it.</blockquote><a href="/state/hipponion">Hipponion</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mamertines were Campanian mercenaries who had served <b>Agathocles of Syracuse</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mamertine-messana">Mamertine Messana</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Agathocles of Syracuse</b> massacred many citizens in 307 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/segesta">Segesta</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Himera, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,13.82&amp;year=-315" aria-label="Himera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Agathocles of Syracuse"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5923 4265h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5923 4265h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,13.82&amp;year=-315">What was here in 315 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
